# Project-local exclusion rules.
#
# These used to live only in `.git/info/exclude`, which is a *private* file: it
# does not travel with the repository, so a fresh clone had no protection at
# all. That mattered once the search index appeared — `store/cache/
# web_search_index.db` is ~341 MB, and an unwary `git add` would have committed
# it. Anything a collaborator also needs ignored belongs here instead.
#
# NOTE: `work/` is ignored by these local rules. Two things inside it are
# deliberately tracked with `git add -f`: the release notes and
# `work/contract_snapshots/`, which the contract tests compare against. The
# rest — checkpoint ledgers, audit reports, verifier output, performance
# scratch — is development process, not something a clone needs.

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
.venv/

# Local stores and their regenerable projections.
store/
store-v1/
sekaisync.db

# Regenerable search index (searchindex.py); several hundred MB.
store/cache/

work/

settings.json.legacy
# Local working copy of settings.json, holding real instance endpoints.
#
# The tracked `settings.json` is a skeleton: the two backend-class entries keep
# their full field structure but every endpoint is empty, and the README shows
# `<placeholder>` values. Real endpoints are a local test/production choice and
# must not reach the remote — publishing one instance's address would read as
# an endorsement of that instance, which is not what the project promises
# (README: "does not imply that SekaiSync endorses or suggests connecting to
# any particular instance").
#
# `settings.json` itself also carries the skip-worktree flag, so a local edit
# there is neither committed nor clobbered by checkout. This rule covers the
# extra copies a workspace may keep.
settings.local.json
settings.*.local.json
docs/
HANDOFF.md
.tmp-test/

# Generated analysis artifacts under data/.
#
# `data/` holds two very different things. Only term-annotations.json is an
# input: the maintainer's manual segmentation of Simplified Chinese story text,
# read at runtime by sekaisync.zhfirst and reproducible by nobody. Everything
# else there is derived from a local store — noun tables exported from terms,
# audit reports over those tables, weight tables and the generated baseline
# block inside BASELINE.md. They are rebuildable by re-running the scripts
# against a store you built yourself, they run to ~18 MB, and they are exports
# of game-derived data, which this repository states it does not bundle
# (data/reports/BASELINE.md: "本仓库不捆绑任何游戏数据").
#
# The files stay on disk; the scripts keep working locally.
data/**
!data/term-annotations.json

# One-off repository surgery: carved the desktop front-end out into its own
# repo. The job is done, the script carries author-machine paths as defaults,
# and nothing imports the seed tree it copies from. Kept on disk for local
# reuse; not part of what a clone needs.
scripts/repo-split/

# The desktop client lives in its own repository (omoinoki/sekaisync-winui3) and
# is meant to be mounted here as a submodule. Only its build output is ignored:
# a path-wide rule would make `git submodule add frontends/WinUI3` silently do
# nothing, because ignore rules are consulted before the submodule is recorded.
frontends/WinUI3/bin/
frontends/WinUI3/obj/

.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.coverage
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
htmlcov/
*.log
